WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.600
A shopper buys a cooler on a retailer's website. The retailer never touches it. It ships from our client's warehouse straight to the shopper. That's drop-ship.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.925
If the ship notice is late or wrong, the shopper has no tracking, and the client's scorecard drops.

00:00:15.925 --> 00:00:20.275
Think of a food delivery app. You order in the app, and a restaurant cooks.

00:00:20.275 --> 00:00:28.950
The app never cooks, but it judges the restaurant on speed, and hides the ones that keep cancelling. Drop-ship platforms work like that app.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:37.376
Compared with bulk, the ship-to is a home address, orders are one or two units, and you must ship fast, often within one to three days.

00:00:37.376 --> 00:00:44.326
The documents change. The eight fifty six carries a tracking number for each package, and the eight ten bills it.

00:00:44.326 --> 00:00:52.526
The eight forty six inventory feed is critical. Drop-ship sells what you say you have. A stale feed means cancelled orders.

00:00:52.526 --> 00:01:02.026
Cancellations travel differently per partner: an eight seventy, or a cancel line inside the eight fifty six. Never assume. Read the guide.

00:01:02.026 --> 00:01:11.576
Many retailers use a platform in the middle: Rithum, formed from CommerceHub and ChannelAdvisor, with its OrderStream and Dsco platforms.

00:01:11.576 --> 00:01:18.576
So the flow is: retailer, then Rithum, then Orderful, then the client's system, like NetSuite or Shopify.

00:01:18.576 --> 00:01:26.551
Now the biggest trap. The platform accepts your file. Delivered, nine ninety seven accepted. All green in Orderful.

00:01:26.551 --> 00:01:33.351
Then it rejects it at the business level: a wrong tracking format, a cancelled line, or a quantity mismatch.

00:01:33.351 --> 00:01:43.776
That rejection lives in the platform's File Error Log and emails, not in Orderful. So check the log after every test, and make sure someone reads those emails.

00:01:43.776 --> 00:01:50.776
Each partner has special rules. One retailer used no eight fifty five, only the nine ninety seven.

00:01:50.776 --> 00:01:59.626
It used fill-or-kill: ship the whole open quantity of a line, or cancel all of it, in one submission. And ship before you invoice.

00:01:59.626 --> 00:02:07.826
Drop-ship also adds a packing slip in the box, with the retailer's branding. Its data must match the order and the ship notice.

00:02:07.826 --> 00:02:16.951
A real lesson. Three test orders reached the client's store marked shipping not required. The store closed them, so no ship notice could be made.

00:02:16.951 --> 00:02:22.801
The fix was the item setting. So check the test items before you ask for test orders.

00:02:22.801 --> 00:02:30.351
And once the platform activates the account, every order is a real customer order. There is no second rehearsal.

00:02:30.351 --> 00:02:37.351
In one line: keep inventory fresh, tracking right, and check the platform's error log, not just Orderful.

00:02:37.351 --> 00:02:39.726
Next, the troubleshooting runbook.

